garrett (1.8.2) stable; urgency=medium

  * The service can remember its operators' verdicts and show a new case
    the nearest ones. It is off unless asked for: MEMORY=neuron-db in the
    service's configuration file, or --memory neuron-db when installing
    (on Windows, -Memory neuron-db, or GARRETT_MEMORY for the one-line
    installer). The memory is neuron-db, packed into the service bundles
    as it is published. Each verdict is kept as its shape (the verdict,
    the platform, the signal ids) and sealed in the service's database;
    forgetting what was learned forgets it too.
    - The analyst and the decision model are given each remembered verdict
      with how it relates to the case: its platform, its signals, how
      often it was given, and whether it covers the case. A verdict covers
      a case only on the same platform, with exactly the same signals,
      given at least twice and never the other way.
    - A stop the decision model ordered is not remembered as an
      operator's verdict.
    - It runs where the service runs on Node.js (serve.mjs, local.mjs),
      not on the Cloudflare deployment.
  * Any model can be plugged into the service: no service is built in.
    AI_PROVIDER says how the model is spoken to, and AI_BASE_URL is the
    HTTPS address the request is posted to, exactly as given; with no
    address, a binding is used.
    - decision (recommended): a decision model is sent the case and one
      question with the allowed answers, and answers a probability for
      each. The service writes the summary, and reads the probability as
      confidence: high from AI_HIGH_CONFIDENCE (0.85 unless set).
    - chat: a chat model is sent messages and answers in text.
    - custom: your own analyst, as before.
    - An answer is read in whichever of those shapes it comes back, and
      nothing but the allowed answers is ever taken from a model.
    - AI_KEY_HEADER sends the key in a header of your choosing.
    - The earlier settings "openai" and "workers-ai" work as they did.
  * The agent is unchanged.

 -- sentinels <noreply@sentinels.invalid>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 16:10:00 +0000

garrett (1.8.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Installing and running, made harder to tamper with.
    - The one-line installers carry the SHA-256 of the bundle they install
      and of the apt signing key, and install nothing that does not match.
      The apt install asks for this release by its version number.
    - The Windows installers take a release site over HTTPS only and unpack
      in a folder only the system and administrators can change. The
      agent's state folder on Windows is kept private the same way.
    - On a Mac, garrett lives in /Library/Application Support/Garrett, a
      folder only root can change, and its command is no longer on the
      PATH: sudo "/Library/Application Support/Garrett/garrett". The
      installer stops when an earlier install is still under /usr/local:
      remove that one first.
    - The agent starts system tools by their full path, never by a name
      looked up on the PATH.
    - The service reads a request only up to its size limit, sends case
      alerts and model requests over HTTPS only, and follows no redirect
      with them.
  * A web server's shell. A shell with no arguments of its own, started by
    a web server whose own command line shows a download-and-run command,
    is ended like one that carries the command itself. A web server that
    is replaced by a shell under the same process number is judged as that
    shell. On Linux a start the kernel reports goes to the responder at
    once.
  * On Windows, a script handed to Windows Script Host straight out of a
    downloads folder or a just-opened archive is reported, and so is a
    program that runs from a folder named like the Windows folder with a
    space after its name.
  * A program that tries many machines on the network on
    remote-administration ports within a minute is reported.
  * At the network card. IPv6 neighbours are read too. A gateway whose
    hardware address goes back and forth between two machines is reported.
    A neighbour trying port after port is now seen on macOS as well as
    Linux. On Linux and Windows, a keyboard the machine has never had,
    followed within seconds by a command window, is reported.
  * Below the system: a firmware boot entry that was removed, and a
    changed boot order or one-time boot selection, are reported.
  * An enrolled machine runs in the lower of the mode its service allowed
    and MODE= in its configuration file. garrett status shows the mode it
    runs in.
  * garrett cases on an enrolled machine shows the cases the machine saw
    and says which service judges them.
  * Trusting a case keeps what was cleaned up under it from being cleaned
    up again, and every clean-up is said, on the machine and on the
    service.
  * Confirming an attack no longer shares addresses that answer for many
    sites (Cloudflare's and Fastly's published networks) across the fleet.
  * Purging the Debian package removes the apt source and signing key the
    installer added.

 -- sentinels <noreply@sentinels.invalid>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 11:30:00 +0000

garrett (1.7.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * At the network card. Two things on the wire that no program on the
    machine starts are now reported; nothing is done about either, because
    what is wrong is on another machine.
    - Another machine on the network starts answering in place of the
      gateway. The system's table of neighbours is read every two seconds
      (every thirty on a Mac) and the change is reported the moment the
      gateway's hardware address becomes one that already belongs to
      another machine. A new router is learned without a word.
    - On Linux, a machine on the network tries port after port. The first
      packet of each arriving connection attempt is read at the network
      card: who sent it and which port it asked for, never what follows.
      Thirty different ports from one neighbour in a minute are reported.
      Scans from the open internet are not.
  * garrett hardware also says whether the gateway could be read.

 -- sentinels <noreply@sentinels.invalid>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 23:30:00 +0000

garrett (1.6.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Below the system. The agent reads what the machine says about its
    firmware and its boot chain, remembers it, and reports a change that no
    update explains. It only reads; nothing down there is ever changed.
    - Secure Boot (on a Mac, System Integrity Protection) was on and is now
      off.
    - The firmware's list of what to start has a new or changed entry.
    - The code the machine starts from changed: the boot sector of a disk,
      the space behind it where GRUB keeps its core on a BIOS machine, or a
      boot loader file on the firmware's partition, and no boot loader
      update was installed (Linux and Windows).
    - The firmware is older than it was.
    - The security chip's measurement of the firmware changed while the
      firmware still gives the same version and date.
    - On Linux, a kernel module is loaded that is not among the modules
      installed for the running kernel.
    - Windows was restarted with its driver signature checks off.
  * garrett hardware shows what could be read on this machine and whether
    any of it changed since the agent's last reading.
  * More command lines are recognised: a kernel module loaded from a
    temporary or home folder, driver signature checks switched off, the
    system's check of what may run switched off, and the firmware chip
    written from the running system.

 -- sentinels <noreply@sentinels.invalid>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 23:00:00 +0000

garrett (1.5.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Privilege escalation is reported as it happens. On Linux: a root shell
    that an ordinary user's program started without going through su, sudo
    or any other program that grants root, and a root shell that su, passwd
    or a program like them turned into directly, which they never do unless
    they were tampered with. On Windows: a shell running as SYSTEM under a
    program of an ordinary account. These are reported, never ended.
  * A shell under a system helper that starts none is reported: winlogon,
    lsass, ctfmon and WerFault on Windows; the kernel's helper thread and
    the network share sign-in helper on Linux.
  * A new SSH sign-in key added to an account while nobody is signed in over
    SSH is reported. The key is left where it is.
  * More command lines are recognised: the download warning removed from a
    new file and AppleScript downloads on macOS; forfiles, mavinject, a
    silent installer fetched from the internet, a boot setting that hides
    memory and .NET loaders talking to the internet on Windows; a container
    escape through the cgroup release agent on Linux; the keychain copied
    out on macOS; firmware written to a storage drive.
  * Web servers and edge systems: a script served from an uploads folder,
    an object smuggled in a request or a cookie, and a long run of plus
    signs are marked. ColdFusion and phar scripts count as scripts.
  * A scheduled job that downloads from a bare internet address, and a macOS
    login item that carries Apple's name but starts a program from a user's
    folder, are reported when they appear.
  * A program that is not a browser talking to the Telegram bot service is
    weighed like one talking to a paste or file-drop host.
  * A browser started where nobody can see it (off the screen and headless
    or one dot in size, or hidden with an extension loaded) is reported:
    password stealers open one that way to read what it has saved.
  * A remote-control tool that runs from a download folder minutes after it
    arrived is reported, the way a fake helpdesk has its victim start one.
    One installed in the program folders is left alone.
  * A program outside the program folders that asks an outside service for
    the machine's public address has that counted against it.
  * On macOS a script started from Script Editor is treated like one started
    from a document, and a login item that borrows the name of Google's
    updater is reported.
  * The service tells agents which signals it knows, and a newer agent
    keeps back the ones an older service would refuse.

 -- sentinels <noreply@sentinels.invalid>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0000

garrett (1.4.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * A visitor confirmed as an attacker on an edge system stays refused when
    they come back. In 1.4.0 their next request inside five minutes opened
    the case again and took them off the refused list. An operator takes a
    visitor off the list with "trust" or "reopen".
  * The guard for Cloudflare Workers says which setting it needs when the
    service is a Worker on the same account's workers.dev address.

